<h1>Choosing dimensionality-reduction methods from evidence, not preference</h1>
<p class="meta">Y. Gao · evidence frozen {{ frozen.frozen_at[:10] }} (commit {{ (frozen.git_commit or 'none')[:10] }}, {{ '{:,}'.format(frozen.n_files) }} checksummed files) · tables and figures rendered from the frozen artifacts; narrative drafted with AI coding assistance and reviewed by the author</p>
</header>

<p class="abstract"><strong>Summary.</strong> Agent 1 is a lightweight, single-orchestrator agent for numerical data. It inspects the data, chooses preprocessing from the inspected profile, and pilots all ten supported dimensionality-reduction methods on one common cohort. Only methods supported by label-free evidence advance.
On PBMC3k it retained {{ names_of(P.decisions.selected) }} and analysed all {{ '{:,}'.format(P.cohort.selected) }} post-QC cells. On PathMNIST it retained {{ names_of(M.decisions.selected) }} and analysed a stratified subset of {{ '{:,}'.format(M.cohort.selected) }} of the {{ '{:,}'.format(M.cohort.available_post_qc) }} training images; measured scaling set that size.
A first version (v1) produced plausible reports, but an audit showed that its shortlist was partly decided by import-time noise and by a stability score that was not comparable across methods. The version reported here (v2) corrects the measurements, fixes the decision rules before the real-data run, and reports how robust each decision is.</p>

<h2>1 · Problem and contribution</h2>
<p>A dimensionality-reduction workflow should explain why a method advances on the dataset at hand. Rather than selecting methods solely from hard-coded heuristics or LLM recommendations, the agent runs a lightweight pilot benchmark across all supported methods. It evaluates their empirical behavior quantitatively and uses that evidence to decide which methods advance to full-scale analysis.</p>
<p>v2 adds three things to that principle:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Comparable evidence:</strong> fit-only timing, stability measured the same way for every method, local and global structure metrics, and explicit failure diagnostics.</li>
<li><strong>Pre-registered decisions:</strong> weights and thresholds were committed before the real-data run, and the robustness of every decision to reasonable rule changes is reported.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence-based final size:</strong> the final cohort size is derived from measured scaling of the shortlisted methods, with no dataset-specific branch.</li>
</ul>

