{% set Pt = (P.final|selectattr('method', 'equalto', 'tsne')|list)[0] %}{% set Pm = (P.final|selectattr('method', 'equalto', 'mds')|list)[0] %}
{% set Mt = (M.final|selectattr('method', 'equalto', 'tsne')|list)[0] %}{% set Mm = (M.final|selectattr('method', 'equalto', 'mds')|list)[0] %}
<p><strong>What the evidence says.</strong> On both datasets the agent kept one method strong on global structure (MDS) and one strong on local structure (t-SNE), next to the PCA baseline.</p>
{% set Pmd = (P.pilot|selectattr('method', 'equalto', 'mds')|list)[0] %}{% set Mmd = (M.pilot|selectattr('method', 'equalto', 'mds')|list)[0] %}{% set Pts = (P.pilot|selectattr('method', 'equalto', 'tsne')|list)[0] %}{% set Mts = (M.pilot|selectattr('method', 'equalto', 'tsne')|list)[0] %}
<p>MDS had the best pilot Q on both datasets. Two components drove it: the highest global structure of all valid methods (G {{ '%.3f'|format(Pmd.global_structure) }} on PBMC3k, {{ '%.3f'|format(Mmd.global_structure) }} on PathMNIST) and higher subsample stability than t-SNE (S {{ '%.3f'|format(Pmd.stability) }} vs {{ '%.3f'|format(Pts.stability) }}; {{ '%.3f'|format(Mmd.stability) }} vs {{ '%.3f'|format(Mts.stability) }}). Together they outweighed t-SNE's better local recall. t-SNE preserved local neighborhoods best: on the final cohorts its recall was {{ '%.3f'|format(Pt.neighbor_recall) }} vs {{ '%.3f'|format(Pm.neighbor_recall) }} for MDS on PBMC3k, and {{ '%.3f'|format(Mt.neighbor_recall) }} vs {{ '%.3f'|format(Mm.neighbor_recall) }} on PathMNIST. Its global correlation was lower ({{ '%.3f'|format(Pt.global_structure) }} and {{ '%.3f'|format(Mt.global_structure) }}).</p>
<p>UMAP advanced on PathMNIST but not on PBMC3k, where its Q ({{ '%.3f'|format((P.pilot|selectattr('method', 'equalto', 'umap')|list)[0].quality) }}) was below both the 90% threshold and PCA. Diffusion maps on PBMC3k were ineligible: their layout collapsed and the diffusion graph was nearly disconnected (Markov gap {{ '%.0e'|format((P.pilot|selectattr('method', 'equalto', 'diffusion_maps')|list)[0].diagnostics.min_markov_spectral_gap) }}).</p>
{% set proj = (M.size_plan.projections|selectattr('n', 'equalto', M.cohort.selected)|list)[0].per_method.mds.seconds %}
<p>The PathMNIST final size was bounded by MDS. Its fitted time exponent was {{ '%.2f'|format(M.size_plan.scaling_model.mds.time_exponent) }}, so 15,000 images would have exceeded the budgets. The size rule needed its safety factor: MDS took {{ '%.0f'|format(Mm.runtime_seconds) }} s at n = {{ '{:,}'.format(M.cohort.selected) }}, against a point projection of {{ '%.0f'|format(proj) }} s ({{ '%.0f'|format(M.cfg.final.safety_factor * proj) }} s with the 2× safety factor). A likely cause is the cubic eigendecomposition inside the classical start, which grows faster than the probes (at most 4,000 observations) could show; this was not timed separately.</p>
<p>The secondary label silhouettes support the same division of labor on PBMC3k (t-SNE highest). On PathMNIST {% if M.final|selectattr('silhouette', 'lt', 0)|list|length == M.final|length %}they are negative for every finalist: pixel distances do not separate the tissue classes{% else %}they remain low{% endif %}, a limitation of the pixel representation rather than of any one method.</p>
<figure><img src="reports/source/figures/final_embeddings_both.png" style="max-height: 118mm"><figcaption>Figure 3. Final embeddings of the two nonlinear finalists common to both datasets. Class names sit at class medians. Colors are secondary annotations, and visual separation is not evidence of biological or histological truth.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>5 · Robustness and reflection</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Cumulative change (same v2 evidence)</th><th>PBMC3k shortlist</th><th>PathMNIST shortlist</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for a in P.decisions.ablation %}<tr><td>{{ a.step }}</td><td>{{ names_of(a.selected) }}</td><td>{{ names_of(M.decisions.ablation[loop.index0].selected) }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>How stable are the decisions?</strong> Across ten rule variants (other weightings, k = 50, the v1 stability definition, ignoring runtime, and others), these are the selection frequencies:</p>
<ul>
<li>PBMC3k: {% for m, f in P.decisions.sensitivity.selection_frequency.items() %}{{ N[m] }} {{ (100 * f)|round|int }}%{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.</li>
<li>PathMNIST: {% for m, f in M.decisions.sensitivity.selection_frequency.items() %}{{ N[m] }} {{ (100 * f)|round|int }}%{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.</li>
</ul>
<p>PCA, MDS and t-SNE are therefore robust conclusions. UMAP's place on PathMNIST is moderately robust; any fourth method on PBMC3k would be fragile, and the agent kept none. The ablation shows which rule mattered: moving from v1's stability definition to subsample stability replaced Isomap by MDS on PBMC3k and by UMAP on PathMNIST.</p>
<p><strong>What v1 got wrong, and what I learned.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Measure before you decide.</strong> {% set Ppc = (P.pilot|selectattr('method', 'equalto', 'pca')|list)[0] %}v1's runtimes were dominated by Python imports. v2 measures PCA's pilot fit at {{ '%.3f'|format(Ppc.runtime_seconds) }} s, while v1 recorded 1.81 s on PBMC3k (examples/v1). A strict Pareto rule then treated sub-second noise as evidence: two v1 runs on the same input, with identical qualities, retained different fourth methods. Perturbing one runtime by ±0.2 s gave six different PathMNIST shortlists.</li>
<li><strong>A shared metric must mean the same thing for every method.</strong> t-SNE with PCA initialization ignores its seed. v1's "seed stability" therefore gave it a measured 1.0, the same score deterministic methods received by definition, while UMAP, MDS and GPLVM were penalized.</li>
<li><strong>The pilot configuration is part of the evidence.</strong> MDS started from random positions stopped at its iteration limit on PBMC3k and looked weak. Started from the classical solution, it converges ({{ (P.pilot|selectattr('method', 'equalto', 'mds')|list)[0].diagnostics.smacof_iterations|max }} SMACOF iterations on the PBMC3k pilot, limit 1,000) and is the best method on both datasets. An evidence-based agent is only as fair as the candidates' default settings.</li>
<li><strong>Diagnostics are necessary but incomplete.</strong> The collapse check, calibrated on simulations before the real-data run, caught the collapsed diffusion maps on PBMC3k. {% set Mll = (M.pilot|selectattr('method', 'equalto', 'lle')|list)[0] %}{% set Mlp = (M.pilot|selectattr('method', 'equalto', 'laplacian')|list)[0] %}Visually degenerate LLE and Laplacian layouts on PathMNIST have collapse ratios of {{ '%.3f'|format(Mll.collapse_ratio) }} and {{ '%.3f'|format(Mlp.collapse_ratio) }}, just above the 0.05 threshold. They were excluded only because their Q was lower.</li>
</ul>

<h2>6 · Strengths, limitations and reproducibility</h2>
<p><strong>Strengths.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>All ten methods are genuine implementations and are attempted on one cohort.</li>
<li>Decisions are pre-registered, machine-readable and recomputed by the validator.</li>
<li>Failures and weak methods stay visible.</li>
<li>One interface adapts preprocessing and cohort size to sparse counts and dense images.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Limitations.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Q is one reasonable weighting. The robustness analysis bounds this choice but does not remove it.</li>
<li>Metrics are in-cohort descriptions, not held-out estimates.</li>
<li>Timing comes from single-threaded runs on one node.</li>
<li>GPLVM uses a fixed epoch budget.</li>
<li>PBMC annotations come from an earlier clustering, and PathMNIST pixel space does not encode histological semantics.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Reproducibility.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The exact environment is given by explicit conda and pip locks.</li>
<li>Every stage records configuration and code hashes, the git commit, host and job.</li>
<li>Cohort IDs, seeds and checksums are saved.</li>
<li><code>freeze</code> locked the {{ '{:,}'.format(frozen.n_files) }} output files before this report was rendered.</li>
<li>Rerun with <code>sbatch scripts/slurm/run_all.sbatch config/&lt;dataset&gt;.yaml</code>.</li>
</ul>

